{
  "abstract": "Can a short training run make Activation Oracle readers forecast what a model writes next, beyond what the visible text gives? Earlier work found that two Qwen3-8B readers (GRPO step 500 and three-layer), read through their trained continuation prompt at one end-of-window activation, beat the window text on imminent backtracking but not on properties of the next sentence. We continue each reader's LoRA for 500 steps on the traces' realised next 48 tokens, using 3,600 cuts from 40 training problems. On development items, sampled forecasts of three next-sentence properties then beat the base model continuing the 50-token window text by +0.075 and +0.091 AUC, and an identically trained no-activation arm stays at chance. We registered four tests and froze the analysis code before generating a fresh set of 30 new problems (700 items), and ran each condition once. The headline test is not confirmed for either reader: trained reader minus window text is +0.004 [−0.029, +0.039] and +0.025 [−0.009, +0.061]. Window-text sampling scored higher on the new problems (0.735 against 0.681). The trained head does depend on the activation: the no-activation arm is 0.28-0.30 lower, and window-only activations lower the same weights by 0.057-0.059 (both confirmed). For the next token, training helps: sampled backtracking beats window text by +0.091 / +0.094, and in a post-hoc teacher-forced comparison the untrained readers again exceed the window's next-token probability (+0.054 / +0.049) while trained readers exceed it by +0.10 to +0.11. In an exploratory test, the readers' Yes/No answer to \"Will the model write the number N next?\" ranks their own forecast claims no better than chance (AUC 0.45-0.46, intervals include 0.5), while agreement among their samples ranks them at 0.70-0.73. Limits: one model, lexical labels, one seed and training budget, and the training arm was chosen on development data.",
  "author": "orthant_cartographer",
  "change_summary": "Corrections from the v1 review; no new experiments. (1) Negative result stated statistically: 'not confirmed' / 'no detected advantage'; the three-layer interval permits ~0.06; the baseline difference between sets is an observation, not an explanation. (2) Development REAL-WIN was retrained on window-only activations, final G4 evaluates REAL weights on them; stated as related but different interventions. (3) Yes/No verification: 'no better than chance' (intervals include 0.5); differences from the verifier of [6] listed; Torrielli et al. credited for sample agreement. (4) Operational sentence-label rules and pointwise, single-seed intervals stated. (5) Reproduction: training activations come from e51_run.py (now included); analyze_final2.py is a portability-edited copy of the frozen file (original sha256 prefix 5f35ce7e).",
